WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.876 --> 00:00:01.960
Oh God!

00:00:02.044 --> 00:00:04.254
No, no. My god, no.
This is hostility.

00:00:04.338 --> 00:00:05.881
I only wrote
the one chapter.

00:00:05.964 --> 00:00:08.342
Well, that's
a hell of a chapter.

00:00:08.425 --> 00:00:09.718
Did you use really big font?

00:00:17.226 --> 00:00:18.227
Hello?

00:00:22.272 --> 00:00:23.315
Mr Duchovny?

00:00:29.321 --> 00:00:30.322
Hello?

00:00:35.244 --> 00:00:36.245
David?

00:00:38.205 --> 00:00:39.206
Hello?

00:00:48.298 --> 00:00:49.675
What the actual fuck?

00:00:52.511 --> 00:00:53.637
Hey.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.347
Hi. Hi!

00:00:58.559 --> 00:00:59.977
I didn't know
anybody was here.

00:01:01.103 --> 00:01:01.937
Ji-Yoon?

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:03.021
Yeah.

00:01:03.689 --> 00:01:05.357
The… the door was open.

00:01:05.858 --> 00:01:07.192
You always leave
your door open?

00:01:07.276 --> 00:01:08.902
Never had a problem before.

00:01:09.653 --> 00:01:10.821
That's why I love it here.

00:01:11.405 --> 00:01:12.573
Nobody bothers you.

00:01:18.537 --> 00:01:19.705
Oh God!

00:01:22.499 --> 00:01:23.417
You okay?

00:01:24.668 --> 00:01:27.880
Yeah. Sorry, for a second,
I thought you were naked.

00:01:30.382 --> 00:01:31.383
Interesting.

00:01:32.342 --> 00:01:34.094
For the distinguished
lectureship,

00:01:34.178 --> 00:01:36.805
I was thinking of presenting
my Yale dissertation.

00:01:37.681 --> 00:01:39.558
"The Schizophrenic Critique
of Pure Reason

00:01:39.641 --> 00:01:41.143
in Beckett's Early Novels".

00:01:41.226 --> 00:01:42.769
I only wrote
the one chapter.

00:01:42.853 --> 00:01:45.189
Well, that's
a hell of a chapter.

00:01:45.272 --> 00:01:46.773
Did you use really big font?

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:48.901
Nope.

00:01:48.984 --> 00:01:50.694
Yeah, I got sidetracked
for a few decades,

00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:52.696
but I've been thinking
about finishing it,

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:55.282
you know,
and filing for my PhD.

00:01:55.908 --> 00:01:57.326
Dr David Duchovny.

00:01:57.409 --> 00:01:58.911
Maybe too much alliteration.

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:01.872
Do you think it's unfair

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:05.542
for people to start thinking
of me as Dr David Duchovny

00:02:05.626 --> 00:02:06.752
after thinking of me as

00:02:06.835 --> 00:02:08.837
just David Duchovny
for so many years?

00:02:12.424 --> 00:02:13.634
Anyway, take a gander.

00:02:13.717 --> 00:02:16.845
I reread it last night.
It's… it's pretty good.

00:02:16.929 --> 00:02:17.971
Press-ient even.

00:02:18.055 --> 00:02:19.515
Press-ient, really?

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:23.310
I think it's pronounced
prescient,

00:02:23.393 --> 00:02:24.561
unless we're English.

00:02:24.645 --> 00:02:26.438
Aren't we speaking English?

00:02:26.522 --> 00:02:29.149
Okay, maybe you chose
the wrong department.

00:02:29.233 --> 00:02:32.569
Maybe you're better suited
for the sch-iences.

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:38.283
I don't say that
because you're Asian.

00:02:39.201 --> 00:02:41.328
I mean, I don't even know
that you are Asian.

00:02:41.411 --> 00:02:42.996
And I don't see colour…

00:02:44.248 --> 00:02:47.835
or ethnicity or even faces…

00:02:48.335 --> 00:02:49.795
at this point, I just see…

00:02:50.337 --> 00:02:53.048
vibe and aura,
and yours is lovely.

00:02:58.262 --> 00:02:59.596
I'm going to make
a sch-moothie.

00:03:01.098 --> 00:03:02.724
-Like a sch-moothie?
-No, thank you.

00:03:03.308 --> 00:03:05.060
Have you been to
the farmers' market here?

00:03:05.894 --> 00:03:06.854
No.

00:03:06.937 --> 00:03:09.898
Ah, I used to think this
town was devoid of culture,

00:03:09.982 --> 00:03:11.984
but I stand corrected.

00:03:12.067 --> 00:03:13.777
I mean, look at that carrot.

00:03:14.778 --> 00:03:16.989
Anyway,
pretend I'm not here.

00:03:24.705 --> 00:03:27.666
I can't just catch you up
with a quick tutorial.

00:03:27.749 --> 00:03:28.584
Why not?

00:03:29.668 --> 00:03:31.670
Because that's
an insane request.

00:03:31.753 --> 00:03:33.755
This is over 30 years old.

00:03:33.839 --> 00:03:35.591
A lot has happened
in the last 30 years.

00:03:35.674 --> 00:03:37.050
Like what?

00:03:38.635 --> 00:03:40.971
Like affect theory,
ecocriticism,

00:03:41.054 --> 00:03:43.015
digital humanities,
new materialism,

00:03:43.098 --> 00:03:44.057
book history,

00:03:44.141 --> 00:03:46.351
gender studies
and critical race theory.

00:03:46.435 --> 00:03:48.854
When did you last pick up
an academic journal?

00:03:48.937 --> 00:03:51.398
Well, I've been on tour
with my band.

00:03:51.481 --> 00:03:53.025
-So…
-It shows.

00:03:53.692 --> 00:03:54.902
And you know what else?

00:03:54.985 --> 00:03:57.487
The fact that you're using
this honour from Pembroke

00:03:57.571 --> 00:03:59.323
to dust off
your dissertation

00:03:59.406 --> 00:04:00.908
and go back
and get your PhD,

00:04:00.991 --> 00:04:02.868
it's… it's…
it's self-serving.

00:04:02.951 --> 00:04:04.995
Whoa, is this hostility

00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:07.706
because Pembroke is like
this lower-tier Ivy

00:04:07.789 --> 00:04:08.790
and I went to Princeton?

00:04:08.874 --> 00:04:09.875
-No!
-And Yale?

00:04:09.958 --> 00:04:12.127
No, no. My God, no.
This is hostility

00:04:12.211 --> 00:04:14.171
because I promised
this lectureship

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:15.631
to someone who deserves it.

00:04:16.256 --> 00:04:17.716
The purpose of the talk
is to present

00:04:17.799 --> 00:04:18.800
cutting-edge scholarship.

00:04:18.884 --> 00:04:20.761
This reads like
it's out of the mid-80s.

00:04:20.844 --> 00:04:21.929
I mean, like the…

00:04:23.347 --> 00:04:25.057
The discipline
has moved forward and…

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:27.017
you're still stuck
back in a different era.

00:04:32.147 --> 00:04:33.357
I just need a minute.

00:04:38.946 --> 00:04:40.531
-Here, can I make a--
-No!

00:04:50.624 --> 00:04:52.376
Your maps and geography

00:04:55.546 --> 00:04:58.131
When I come back
If I come back

00:04:58.215 --> 00:05:00.425
I swear that I'll be simpler

00:05:01.885 --> 00:05:04.638
When I come back
If I come back

00:05:04.721 --> 00:05:06.682
I'll have a mind of winter

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:09.893
I'll have a mind of winter

00:05:11.019 --> 00:05:14.064
I'll have a mind of winter

00:05:15.774 --> 00:05:18.151
Only song I've ever heard
quote Wallace Stevens.

00:05:18.235 --> 00:05:19.611
Fuck, I hope so.

00:05:22.239 --> 00:05:23.323
You have a good voice.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:35.043
Was there someone else
before me?

00:05:35.127 --> 00:05:36.962
A celebrity author?

00:05:37.045 --> 00:05:38.755
Did Pembroke offer
this position

00:05:38.839 --> 00:05:40.507
to someone else first?

00:05:42.551 --> 00:05:44.178
It's all right,
I can take it.

00:05:47.472 --> 00:05:49.558
There was talk of
James Franco a while back.

00:05:50.184 --> 00:05:52.269
Well, he got his PhD
from Yale.

00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:54.771
-Did he? He got it?
-Yeah. He did.

00:05:54.855 --> 00:05:55.772
He did.

00:05:56.565 --> 00:05:58.108
They might have invited
Ethan Hawke,

00:05:58.192 --> 00:05:59.818
but he was shooting
in Bulgaria.

00:06:00.694 --> 00:06:01.945
What was he shooting?

00:06:02.029 --> 00:06:03.155
-Pardon me?
-In Bulgaria?

00:06:03.655 --> 00:06:06.033
Well, a movie
or a TV series?

00:06:06.116 --> 00:06:09.161
Well, I don't know
what he was shooting.

00:06:09.244 --> 00:06:10.829
There's a lot of shooting
in Bulgaria.

00:06:11.371 --> 00:06:12.372
Okay.

00:06:15.626 --> 00:06:16.752
Look, so…

00:06:18.003 --> 00:06:19.713
I did all that work at Yale
on Beckett.

00:06:19.796 --> 00:06:22.549
Right? But I figured
I'll just stick with that

00:06:22.633 --> 00:06:25.093
and then…
Let's take a swim.

00:06:25.177 --> 00:06:27.930
Oh, I don't have
a bathing suit.

00:06:28.013 --> 00:06:29.181
Well, you can borrow one.

00:06:29.848 --> 00:06:31.099
Here, borrow this one.

00:06:32.184 --> 00:06:34.311
Who's… Who wore that?

00:06:34.394 --> 00:06:36.522
That's mine.
It's a speed suit.

00:06:36.605 --> 00:06:38.482
I wear it when
I wanna swim for time.

00:06:38.565 --> 00:06:40.275
Because skin's a drag,
because of hair.

00:06:40.359 --> 00:06:42.569
You can't teach Beckett for
the rest of the semester.

00:06:42.653 --> 00:06:45.280
Why not? You got Murphy,
Malloy, Malone Dies.

00:06:45.364 --> 00:06:46.907
The students bought
the other books.

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:48.867
So I'll reimburse them.

00:06:48.951 --> 00:06:51.495
I'm a grossly overpaid
actor-slash-musician

00:06:51.578 --> 00:06:53.789
slash-novelist
and they're students,

00:06:54.373 --> 00:06:55.666
burdened with student debt.

00:06:55.749 --> 00:06:57.501
-I will unburden them.
-I mean…

00:06:57.584 --> 00:06:59.878
The syllabus is a contract
You can't just change--

00:06:59.962 --> 00:07:01.213
Let's go for a swim.

00:07:01.296 --> 00:07:03.882
You know what?
Teaching is not a pastime,

00:07:03.966 --> 00:07:05.133
it's a profession.

00:07:05.217 --> 00:07:07.761
If you really want to make
a contribution to the field,

00:07:07.845 --> 00:07:10.013
tell them you've changed
your mind

00:07:10.097 --> 00:07:12.933
give them a cheque to endow
a chair in your name,

00:07:13.016 --> 00:07:14.643
and I'll get you
an honorary doctorate.

00:07:14.726 --> 00:07:17.396
The David Duchovny Chair
in English Studies?

00:07:17.479 --> 00:07:21.024
The Dr David Duchovny Chair
in English Studies.

